# Inline single-use functions

A function used once in the same file is inlined at that call site. The inlined block gets a comment saying what the number or the step is for.

```python
# BAD — named only to be called once
def _chunk_size(rate: int) -> int:
    return max(1, int(rate * 0.05))

frames = stream.read(_chunk_size(rate))

# GOOD — the number stays next to the read, with why it exists
# 0.05 seconds of audio per read. Abort is checked 20 times a second.
frames = stream.read(max(1, int(rate * 0.05)))
```

A one-line wrapper is always inlined.

```python
# BAD — the method exists only to forward the call
def play(self, clip, abort):
    self._play(clip.samples, clip.sample_rate, abort)

# GOOD — the caller uses the playback loop
self.play(clip.samples, clip.sample_rate, abort)
```

A real algorithm that is called once stays named. Band spectra, DTW, and shortcut key parsing are algorithms, not wrappers.

```python
# GOOD — one caller, but the name is the algorithm
def _dtw_similarity(reference, take, band_ratio):
    ...

similarity = _dtw_similarity(reference, recorded, 0.2)
```
